@packages/compiler/src/evaluation-capabilities.sigil import { SigilAgentCapabilityContract }
@packages/compiler/src/evaluation-execution.sigil import { SigilAgentExecutionPolicy }

component SigilCompilationProfile {
  goal {
    Own built-in and project compiler profiles, evaluator selection, stage
    dependency closure, operational limits, and reproducible effective-profile
    identity for evaluation by any registered adapter implementation.
  }

  interface {
    CompilationProfile {
      resolve(name, toolsCompile, requestedStage?) returns ordered stages,
      evaluators, skills, capabilities, execution budgets, resource limits, and an
      effective-profile fingerprint or rejects an unknown profile or stage with
      COMPILER_INVALID_INVOCATION and an unavailable required evaluator with a stable
      profile failure

      standard and critical-system are built in; project profiles may configure or
      omit stages subject to dependency rules

      each evaluator names one nonempty provider identifier; the compiler compares
      and keys on it without interpreting it, and its adapter implementation is
      supplied by the compiler host or a plugin

      each evaluator binds one stable adapter implementation identifier and exact
      implementation version
    }

  }
}

expand SigilCompilationProfile {
  constraints {
    ProfileConfiguration {
      Effective tools.compile configuration may include developer-local overrides
      merged by SigilWorkspaceLoader before profile resolution.

      Local overrides follow the same validation and profile rules as shared
      tools.compile configuration.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompilationProfile {
  logic {
    StageConfiguration {
      A profile may configure an optional stages map from agentic stage identity to
      one or more evaluator identities.

      stages.main is the fallback evaluator binding for every agentic stage without
      an exact stage entry. An exact stage entry takes precedence over main.

      When stages is absent, evaluatorIds retains its existing profile-wide binding
      behavior. Deterministic stages do not require a stage evaluator binding.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    StageConfiguration {
      stages keys are known agentic stage identities or the reserved main fallback.

      Every selected stage evaluator identity is nonempty, unique within its binding,
      and resolves to one configured evaluator with its own provider, adapter
      implementation, and optional model.

      A selected profile must resolve an evaluator binding for every required agentic
      stage before evaluation begins.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    StageScopedEvaluatorBinding {
      Decision: Configure optional profile stages bindings with main as the fallback
      and exact agentic-stage bindings as overrides.

      Scope: Governs evaluator, adapter, and model selection for stages within one
      tools.compile profile; excludes deterministic stages and CLI profile selection.

      Trade-offs: Profiles expose more configuration detail, while one shared main
      binding keeps ordinary profiles concise.

      Design issues addressed: One profile-wide evaluator set cannot select a fast
      model for one review stage and a deeper or different provider for another.

      Discarded alternatives: Per-stage adapter fields, mandatory bindings for every
      stage, and a second profile layer were rejected because they duplicate named
      evaluator configuration or make ordinary profiles unnecessarily verbose.

      Consequences: Each stage report and effective-profile fingerprint identify the
      evaluator bindings selected for that exact stage.
    }
  }

  cases {
    StageConfiguration {
      A semantic-readiness binding overrides main while architecture-design uses
      main.

      A profile without stages continues to use its evaluatorIds binding for every
      agentic stage.

      An agentic stage without an exact or main binding rejects before evaluation.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompilationProfile {
  logic {
    CompilationProfile {
      Merge built-in defaults with the selected tools.compile project profile.

      Resolve execution budgets from tools.compile.budgets and request limits from
      tools.compile.limits. Omitted fields use the compiler's backward-compatible
      defaults.

      Treat the singular adapter as the backward-compatible default evaluator and
      select configured evaluator identities through evaluatorIds.

      Require a nonempty provider identifier on each selected evaluator. Resolve each
      selected evaluator's implementation identifier and exact version, and include
      both in effective-profile identity. Resolve registration availability only for
      selected evaluators.

      Resolve AgentCapabilityContract and AdapterObservabilityDeclaration through
      SigilAgentCapabilityContract. Resolve AgentExecutionBudgets and
      AgentOperationalLimits through SigilAgentExecutionPolicy.

      Validate only selected evaluator identities. Resolve requestedStage to its
      complete transitive dependency closure without enabling unrelated stages.

      Fingerprint effective stages, skills, adapters, models, capabilities, execution
      budgets, resource limits, adapter observability declarations, disabled stages,
      and relevant versions.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    ProfileConfiguration {
      ConfigFile tools.compile contains no credentials or secret values.

      tools.compile.budgets configures execution values and limits configures request
      sizes. Every configured value is a positive safe integer.

      An initial agent-request size limit is an operational transport bound. It does
      not bound evidence that an adapter later reads from the workspace.

      A command-output limit bounds retained nonessential trace content. Exceeding it
      alone does not fail evaluation when the final result remains intact and valid.

      Evaluator identities are unique; evaluatorIds contains no duplicates and
      references configured evaluators.

      Every selected evaluator names a nonempty provider identifier.

      Every selected evaluator declares one nonempty adapter implementation
      identifier and exact version.

      Unselected evaluator configuration does not affect workspace loading or another
      profile.
    }

    StageConfiguration {
      Profiles configure stages subject to declared dependencies. Disabled stages are
      not required for green and remain visible in the report and fingerprint.

      Stage-scoped compilation cannot bypass dependencies or select a disabled stage.
    }

    BuiltInProfiles {
      standard requires deterministic foundation and configured design evaluation
      through one evaluator.

      critical-system adds standards and risk evaluation, at least two distinct
      available evaluator identities, and material-disagreement detection.

      Invalid critical-system evaluator configuration fails only that selected run.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ConfigurableCompilationResources {
      Decision: Make execution budgets and evaluation request limits explicit
      tools.compile configuration with backward-compatible omitted-field defaults.

      Scope: Governs compiler profile resolution, adapter request validation, and
      compilation resource limits; excludes provider credentials and semantic
      evaluation policy.

      Trade-offs: Workspaces can size compilation for their available resources,
      while effective profiles retain the resolved values for auditability and
      reproducibility.

      Design issues addressed: Hidden source-level constants made resource policy
      vary without a visible workspace contract and prevented operators from
      adjusting safe limits for larger repositories.

      Consequences: Configuration changes alter the effective-profile fingerprint and
      can change acceptance of large requests and evaluator execution.
    }

    OpaqueProviderIdentity {
      Decision: Treat the evaluator provider as an opaque identifier owned by the
      adapter package that declares it, requiring only that it is nonempty.

      Scope: Governs tools.compile evaluator configuration and effective-profile
      identity; excludes adapter implementation trust and provider protocol mapping.

      Trade-offs: A misspelled provider is reported when registration resolves rather
      than when configuration parses, in exchange for adding a provider without
      changing this contract.

      Design issues addressed: A closed provider list made the compiler aware of
      every supported provider, so each new adapter required a compiler change.

      Discarded alternatives: Keeping a closed list was rejected because it couples a
      provider-neutral compiler to the set of adapters that happen to exist.

      Consequences: Profile fingerprints identify the configured provider name
      together with the explicitly selected implementation identity and version.
    }

    ExplicitAdapterImplementationSelection {
      Decision: Require each evaluator configuration to select one stable adapter
      implementation identifier and exact version.

      Scope: Governs evaluator configuration and effective-profile identity; excludes
      host or plugin registration matching owned by the adapter execution boundary.

      Trade-offs: Adapter upgrades require an intentional profile change.

      Design issues addressed: A provider name alone cannot identify which host or
      plugin implementation the user accepted.

      Discarded alternatives: Discovery order and implicit host or plugin precedence
      were rejected as nonreproducible.

      Consequences: Equivalent effective profiles bind the same adapter
      implementation identity and version.
    }

    ProfileScopedCompilation {
      Decision: Compute status against an exact named profile and environment
      fingerprint whose stages are configurable.

      Scope: Governs built-in and project profiles.

      Trade-offs: Green is never an unconditional property of a Sigil file.

      Consequences: Evaluation depth remains configurable and auditable.
    }

    StageScopedCompilation {
      Decision: Invoke one named stage with its complete dependency closure and no
      unrelated stages.

      Scope: Governs focused compiler runs and effective-profile identity.
      Implementation alignment runs current-code-compatibility with only its
      deterministic-foundation prerequisite; it excludes semantic-readiness and
      architecture-design.

      Trade-offs: Stage identifiers are reserved compile operands.

      Consequences: Focused evaluation avoids unrelated stage cost and meaning.
      Implementation alignment reports current-code drift without asserting design or
      semantic-readiness findings.
    }

    CriticalEvaluatorRedundancy {
      Decision: Require at least two configured evaluator identities for every
      critical-system agentic stage and detect material disagreement.

      Scope: Governs critical-system profile validation and evaluator selection.

      Trade-offs: Critical runs cost more and may remain non-green on disagreement.

      Discarded alternatives: One evaluator or an extra stage was rejected as false
      redundancy.

      Consequences: Critical status records independent completion and disagreement.
    }
  }

  cases {
    StageConfiguration {
      A disabled optional stage can still permit green and is recorded.

      Requesting semantic-readiness executes deterministic-foundation first and no
      unrelated later stage.

      Requesting implementation alignment executes deterministic-foundation and
      current-code-compatibility without semantic-readiness or architecture-design.
    }

    BuiltInProfiles {
      Missing critical evaluator configuration does not prevent standard compilation.

      Selecting critical-system with fewer than two evaluators rejects with
      COMPILER_PROFILE_EVALUATORS_REQUIRED before agentic evaluation.
    }
  }
}
